  "documentation": {
    "range": "<em>Melanocetus johnsonii</em>&#160;is distributed&#160;from the Atlantic, Pacific and Indian Oceans between 66<sup>o</sup>N and 53<sup>o</sup>S and it has occurred in nets fishing at zero to 2,100 m depth, but mostly shallower than 1,500 m (Pietsch 2009).&#160;Vakily<em>&#160;et al</em>. (a study by FishBase on West African fishes; 2002) report&#160;<em>M. johnsonii&#160;</em>as distributed off the Cape Verde Islands. This species occurred in a trawl survey conducted off the coast of Namibia between the years 1992-1996 (Hamukuaya&#160;<em>et al</em>. 2001).&#160;In the Western Atlantic it is distributed from 175-2,700 m depth (mostly greater than 2,100 m) from Greenland south to Argentina (Moore<em> et al.</em> 2003). One specimen occurred in Icelandic waters at 62°50’N,&#160;30°35’W (Jonsson and Palsson 1999). Near Hawaii, it is known from Oahu and Johnson Atoll from 600-1,000 m depth (Mundy 2005). This species occurs off of Japan from the Pacific coast of Tohoku District and around Okinotorishima Island (Nakabo 2002).",
    "population": "<em>Melanocetus johnsonii </em>is known from at least 852 female specimens and eight males (Pietsch 2009).&#160;Melanocetids are&#160;solitary species that do not occur abundantly (Pietsch in press).<p></p>",
    "habitats": "<em>Melanocetus johnsonii </em>females have a maximum size of 15.4 cm SL,&#160;while males are up to 2.8 cm (Pietsch 2009).&#160;Melanocetids are&#160;deep-sea fishes that inhabit the mesopelagic and bathypelagic zones. They exhibit extreme sexual dimorphism. Females attract prey with an elongate first dorsal spine that has been modified as a lure. Males have highly developed sensory organs that allow them to find females at which point they will temporarily attach themselves to the body (Pietsch in press). Vakily&#160;<em>et al</em>. (2002) describe&#160;<em>M. johnsonii&#160;</em>as bathypelagic with a maximum size of 3 cm TL off of West Africa.",
    "threats": "There are no known major threats to&#160;<em>Melanocetus johnsonii.</em>",
    "measures": "There are no known conservation measures for&#160;<em>Melanocetus johnsonii.</em>",
    "use_trade": "<em>Melanocetus johnsonii</em>&#160;is not utilized&#160;(Pietsch in press).&#160;This species may be caught in trawls off of Namibia (Bianchi&#160;<em>et al.</em>&#160;1999).",
    "rationale": "<em>Melanocetus johnsonii</em>&#160;is circumglobally distributed in the meso-bathypelagic zones. It is a rare, deep-dwelling species. It may occur as bycatch in trawl fisheries, but it is not utilized and there are no major threats. It is therefore listed as Least Concern.&#160;<p><br/></p>",
    "trend_justification": null,
    "taxonomic_notes": null
  },
